  "raw": {
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Plymouth-State-University/Assistant-Professor-of-Practice_JR9715": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Practice, Criminology/Criminal Justice",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The Criminology/Criminal Justice Programs at Plymouth State University invite applications for a full-time, 9-month, non-tenure-track Assistant Professor of Practice in Criminology/Criminal Justice , beginning August 2027. The successful candidate will join a collaborative faculty delivering high-quality face-to-face education in an undergraduate setting. Responsibilities include teaching undergraduate courses, advising and mentoring students, engaging in university and professional service, and working with community partners. The standard workload is 85% teaching, 15% service. PRIORITY REVIEW DEADLINE: 5:00 pm on Friday, October 2, 2026. Applications must be submitted by the date listed above in order to receive priority review. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled or the search is otherwise closed at the University’s discretion. About Criminology/Criminal Justice The Criminology/Criminal Justice programs oversee a Bachelor of Arts in Criminology, a Bachelor of Science in Criminal Justice, and an Applied Bachelor’s in Police Studies. We also provide coursework to Conservation Law Enforcement and Forensic Science majors. Our in-person learning environment emphasizes active engagement, reflection, collaboration, and practical application. Students learn through independent work, group projects, discussion, presentations, and innovative hands-on experiences. Our programs emphasize evidence-informed practice, ethical decision making, and practical skill application. Faculty equip, support, and challenge students to develop the knowledge and skills needed for professional practice. Community partnerships, professional collaborations, and interdisciplinary learning further enrich the educational experience. The Programs regularly seek feedback from students, alumni, adjunct faculty, and community partners and use that feedback, along with other program data, to evaluate and continuously improve our curriculum and educational practices. Duties and Responsibilities The successful candidate will: Teach 15-16 credit hours per semester of undergraduate courses in Criminology/Criminal Justice, including Criminal Law and Criminal Procedure Advise up to 30 undergraduate Criminology, Criminal Justice, Police Studies, and Conservation Law Enforcement students Participate in university, professional, and community service activities Develop and sustain collaborative relationships with community partners throughout New Hampshire Oversee the Criminal Justice internship program Contribute to curriculum development, program evaluation, and continuous improvement Incorporate evidence-informed practices, critical thinking, and real-world professional applications into teaching and student learning Support innovative, experiential, and interdisciplinary approaches to teaching and learning Minimum Qualifications Masters degree in Criminology, Criminal Justice, or a related Discipline JD and ABD will be considered Demonstrated excellence in college-level teaching with evidence from colleagues and students Skills in academic advising and mentoring students Innovative, student-driven, and possessing strong interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills Ability to teach multiple core courses in the criminology and criminal justice program Ability to contribute to program and university service, and to engage with the discipline and the community Additional Desirable Qualifications PhD in Criminology, Criminal Justice, or a related discipline Expertise in Conservation Law Enforcement and/or Environmental Crime Experience developing or maintaining partnerships with internship sites Experience with experiential, hands-on, or innovative approaches to teaching Experience with interdisciplinary collaboration or community-engaged learning. Applicant Instructions To receive full consideration, all application materials must be submitted through the University's online applicant site. Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - If more than 5 documents are required, upload additional documents within the \"Application Questions\" section.)  Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Statement of Teaching Philosophy Copies of Graduate Transcripts Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration.  Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Compensation Pay Range $55,000.00 - $60,000.00 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. Academic Year (20 Pay Periods) Plymouth State University offers a competitive salary commensurate with experience and a comprehensive benefits package. Plymouth State University offers high-quality baccalaureate, graduate, and doctoral degree programs. Through research and innovative community partnerships, the University contributes to the economic, social, cultural, and environmental development of the North Country and Lakes Region of New Hampshire and beyond. Plymouth State University (PSU) is transforming the campus to create a first-of-its-kind university holistically organized around integrated clusters. Our goal is to provide a dynamic interdisciplinary and innovative learning environment. Students, faculty, staff, and community will connect to solve real-world challenges that will benefit people and organizations at all levels. We are a residential and comprehensive university with a warm and engaged learning community focused on teaching excellence and regional engagement. The University is located in the heart of the Lakes Region and White Mountains of New Hampshire and serves approximately 4,300 undergraduate and more than 2,000 graduate students. Surrounded by lakes and mountains, PSU’s 170-acre campus provides students with an outstanding natural laboratory for learning and an ideal setting for outdoor recreation. PSU has consistently earned national recognition for its commitment to community service, environmental sustainability, academic excellence, and employee wellness. Plymouth State University offers high-quality baccalaureate, graduate, and doctoral degree programs. Through research and innovative community partnerships, the University contributes to the economic, social, cultural, and environmental development of the North Country and Lakes Region of New Hampshire and beyond. Plymouth State University (PSU) is transforming the campus to create a first-of-its-kind university holistically organized around integrated clusters. Our goal is to provide a dynamic interdisciplinary and innovative learning environment. Students, faculty, staff, and community will connect to solve real-world challenges that will benefit people and organizations at all levels. We are a residential and comprehensive university with a warm and engaged learning community focused on teaching excellence and regional engagement. The University is located in the heart of the Lakes Region and White Mountains of New Hampshire and serves approximately 4,300 undergraduate and more than 2,000 graduate students. Surrounded by lakes and mountains, PSU’s 170-acre campus provides students with an outstanding natural laboratory for learning and an ideal setting for outdoor recreation. PSU has consistently earned national recognition for its commitment to community service, environmental sustainability, academic excellence, and employee wellness. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Salary Grade: Faculty 01 Location: Plymouth"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Plymouth-State-University/Assistant-Professor-of-Practice--Physical-Therapy_JR9528": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Practice, Physical Therapy",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 27 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app The Doctor of Physical Therapy (DPT) Program in the School of Health at Plymouth State University seeks a full-time 12-month faculty member at the rank of Assistant Professor of Practice to support delivery of the professional curriculum. This position is primarily responsible for teaching, laboratory instruction, and coordination of selected courses across the curriculum, as well as participation in integrated clinical education experiences The faculty member will contribute to a highly integrated, practice-oriented curriculum that combines didactic instruction with early and progressive clinical experiences, including participation in the program’s pro bono clinic. Responsibilities include course coordination, student assessment, and engagement in program assessment and continuous improvement processes consistent with accreditation standards. The position requires maintenance of contemporary clinical expertise and engagement in scholarship appropriate to a teaching-focused faculty role. PRIORITY REVIEW DEADLINE: 5:00 pm on Friday, October 2, 2026. Applications must be submitted by the date listed above in order to receive priority review. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled or the search is otherwise closed at the University’s discretion. Duties/Responsibilities Teaching and Course Coordination - 70% Serve as primary instructor and/or course coordinator for assigned courses, including but not limited to Clinical & Functional Anatomy, Musculoskeletal Conditions, Patient/Client Management V, and Movement Systems Deliver classroom and laboratory instruction emphasizing clinical reasoning, examination, and intervention skills Develop and maintain course materials, assessments, and learning activities aligned with program outcomes and accreditation standards Evaluate student performance and provide timely, constructive feedback Provide advising and mentoring for Doctor of Physical Therapy (DPT) students and undergraduate Allied Health students as assigned Profession, University, School and Program Service - 20% Participate in program assessment, curriculum review, and strategic planning processes Contribute to accreditation-related activities and documentation Engage in faculty meetings and program initiatives Participate in professional, university, school, and community service activities, including shared governance and engagement with professional organizations Clinical Scholarship - 10% Engage in clinical scholarship that advances the profession of physical therapy, including integration of evidence into teaching and dissemination of clinical expertise Maintain contemporary expertise through clinical practice, continuing education, and professional engagement Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Doctor of Physical Therapy (DPT) or equivalent entry-level physical therapy degree Licensed or eligible for licensure as a physical therapist in the State of New Hampshire Completion of, or Progress toward, a terminal academic doctoral degree (e.g., PhD, EdD, ScD) consistent with programmatic needs and accreditation requirement Minimum of 8 years of contemporary clinical experience in physical therapy practice Demonstrated effectiveness in teaching in a graduate professional physical therapy education setting, including classroom and laboratory instruction Demonstrated teaching experience in areas including but not limited to clinical anatomy, orthopedic pathology, and management of musculoskeletal, complex and cardiopulmonary cases. Required Licenses & Certifications: Licensed to practice Physical Therapy in the State of New Hampshire Preferred Qualifications: Board certification in a clinical specialty relevant for the expected teaching assignment through the American Board of Physical Therapy Specialties (ABPTS) (e.g., Orthopedic or Cardiopulmonary Clinical Specialist) Skills & Competencies: Strong clinical reasoning and problem-solving skills in musculoskeletal and cardiopulmonary physical therapy practice Ability to effectively communicate complex concepts in both classroom and laboratory settings Strong organizational, time management, and interpersonal skills Ability to mentor, instruct, and evaluate undergraduate and graduate-level students in academic and clinical environments Ability to integrate evidence-based practice and contemporary clinical knowledge into teaching and learning activities Commitment to inclusive, student-centered learning environments Ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, and clinical partners Ability to adapt teaching strategies to support diverse learners and dynamic educational settings Ability to perform the physical tasks associated with laboratory instruction and clinical skills demonstration Applicant Instructions:  Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents) Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Compensation Pay Range: $95,000 - $100,000 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications. Plymouth State University offers high-quality baccalaureate, graduate, and doctoral degree programs. Through research and innovative community partnerships, the University contributes to the economic, social, cultural, and environmental development of the North Country and Lakes Region of New Hampshire and beyond. Plymouth State University (PSU) is transforming the campus to create a first-of-its-kind university holistically organized around integrated clusters. Our goal is to provide a dynamic interdisciplinary and innovative learning environment. Students, faculty, staff, and community will connect to solve real-world challenges that will benefit people and organizations at all levels. We are a residential and comprehensive university with a warm and engaged learning community focused on teaching excellence and regional engagement. The University is located in the heart of the Lakes Region and White Mountains of New Hampshire and serves approximately 4,300 undergraduate and more than 2,000 graduate students. Surrounded by lakes and mountains, PSU’s 170-acre campus provides students with an outstanding natural laboratory for learning and an ideal setting for outdoor recreation. PSU has consistently earned national recognition for its commitment to community service, environmental sustainability, academic excellence, and employee wellness. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Salary Grade: Faculty 01 Location: Plymouth"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Plymouth-State-University/Assistant-Associate-Professor_JR9676": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Clinical Mental Health Counseling",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 27 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app Tenure-Track Assistant or Associate Professor of Clinical Mental Health Counseling Plymouth State University | Plymouth, New Hampshire PRIORITY REVIEW DEADLINE: 5:00 pm on Friday, October 9, 2026. Applications must be submitted by the date listed above in order to receive priority review. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled or the search is otherwise closed at the University’s discretion. The Counselor Education and School Psychology (CESP) Programs at Plymouth State University invite applications for a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor of Clinical Mental Health Counseling , beginning August 2027. The successful candidate will join a collaborative faculty committed to preparing ethical, reflective, and skilled helping professionals. Responsibilities include teaching graduate courses, advising and mentoring students, engaging in university and professional service, and developing and maintaining an active research and scholarship agenda consistent with the Boyer Model. The standard workload is 70% teaching, 15% service, and 15% research and scholarship . Summer teaching on an overload basis is optional. About Counselor Education and School Psychology The Counselor Education and School Psychology Programs prepare future helping professionals to enter their chosen professions with confidence, ethical grounding, strong critical-thinking skills, and a commitment to lifelong growth. Our programs emphasize evidence-informed practice, personal and professional wellness, advocacy, culturally responsive practice, and collaborative goal setting that honors the personal and cultural contexts, values, and beliefs of those we serve. CESP faculty equip, support, and challenge students to develop the knowledge and skills needed for professional practice in a rapidly changing world. We integrate evidence-informed and data-driven decision making with real-world professional and ethical applications, while emphasizing personal growth and a wellness-oriented mindset throughout the curriculum. Our predominantly in-person learning environment emphasizes active engagement, reflection, collaboration, and practical application. Students learn through independent work, group projects, discussion, presentations, and innovative hands-on experiences, including the use of actors and pre-practicum field experiences. Faculty mentor students throughout their graduate experience and strive to create a learning environment that recognizes individual strengths, encourages growth, and challenges students to reach their highest potential. Community partnerships, professional collaborations, and interdisciplinary learning further enrich the educational experience. The Programs regularly seek feedback from students, alumni, adjunct faculty, and community partners and use that feedback, along with other program data, to evaluate and continuously improve our curriculum and educational practices. The CESP Programs offer master's, Education Specialist, and professional certification programs with concentrations in Clinical Mental Health Counseling, School Counseling, and School Psychology . The Counselor Education Programs enroll more than 100 students across the Clinical Mental Health Counseling, School Counseling programs and Dual program (CMHC and SC). Both programs are CACREP-accredited , and the School Counseling Program is currently the only CACREP-accredited school counseling program in New Hampshire. Additional certificate offerings include play therapy, addictions treatment, and organizational leadership. Duties and Responsibilities The successful candidate will: Teach graduate courses in the Counselor Education Programs, including courses in the CACREP core and Clinical Mental Health Counseling specialization. Advise, mentor, and support graduate counseling students throughout their academic and professional development. Establish and maintain an active program of research and scholarship consistent with the Boyer Model. Participate in university, professional, and community service activities. Develop and sustain collaborative relationships with counselors, clinical mental health agencies, and other community partners throughout New Hampshire. Contribute to curriculum development, program evaluation, and continuous improvement. Incorporate evidence-informed practices, critical thinking, and real-world professional applications into teaching and student learning. Support innovative, experiential, and interdisciplinary approaches to counselor education. Contribute to a learning environment that promotes personal growth, wellness, ethical practice, advocacy, and respect for individual and cultural differences. Minimum Qualifications Doctorate in Counselor Education and Supervision from a CACREP-accredited institution, conferred by August 1, 2027. Professional experience and expertise in clinical mental health counseling. NCC eligibility and eligibility for clinical mental health counseling licensure. Demonstrated potential for excellence in graduate teaching. Demonstrated ability or potential to establish an active research and scholarship agenda. Strong interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills. Demonstrated ability to advise, mentor, and support graduate students. Understanding and commitment to ethical and culturally responsive counseling practice. Commitment to social justice, advocacy, and respect for diverse perspectives, identities, values, and lived experiences. Competence with instructional technology and innovative course delivery strategies. Commitment to evidence-informed practice, critical thinking, and continuous professional growth. Expertise and professional experience in clinical mental health counseling are required . Additional Desirable Qualifications Two or more years of successful graduate-level teaching experience. Active involvement in professional counseling organizations. Established research agenda with evidence of scholarly productivity and peer-reviewed publications. School counseling experience and/or expertise working with children and adolescents. Experience developing or maintaining partnerships with clinical mental health agencies or community organizations. Experience with experiential, hands-on, or innovative approaches to counselor education. Experience with interdisciplinary collaboration or community-engaged learning. Experience as a school counselor is preferred. Applicant Instructions To receive full consideration, all application materials must be submitted through the University's online applicant site. Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents - If more than 5 documents are required, upload additional documents within the \"Application Questions\" section.)  Resume/CV Cover Letter Contact Information for 3 Professional References Statement of Teaching Philosophy Curriculum Vitae Statement of Research and Scholarship Agenda Copies of Graduate Transcripts Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration.  Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Compensation Pay Range $68,000.00 - $95,000.00 Academic Year (20 Pay Periods) Plymouth State University offers a competitive salary commensurate with experience and a comprehensive benefits package. The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate's qualifications. Plymouth State University offers high-quality baccalaureate, graduate, and doctoral degree programs. Through research and innovative community partnerships, the University contributes to the economic, social, cultural, and environmental development of the North Country and Lakes Region of New Hampshire and beyond. Plymouth State University (PSU) is transforming the campus to create a first-of-its-kind university holistically organized around integrated clusters. Our goal is to provide a dynamic interdisciplinary and innovative learning environment. Students, faculty, staff, and community will connect to solve real-world challenges that will benefit people and organizations at all levels. We are a residential and comprehensive university with a warm and engaged learning community focused on teaching excellence and regional engagement. The University is located in the heart of the Lakes Region and White Mountains of New Hampshire and serves approximately 4,300 undergraduate and more than 2,000 graduate students. Surrounded by lakes and mountains, PSU’s 170-acre campus provides students with an outstanding natural laboratory for learning and an ideal setting for outdoor recreation. PSU has consistently earned national recognition for its commitment to community service, environmental sustainability, academic excellence, and employee wellness. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Salary Grade: Faculty 01 Location: Plymouth"
    },
    "https://usnh.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Careers/job/Plymouth-State-University/Clinical-Assistant-Associate-Professor_JR9046": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor, Nursing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nUSNH Employees should apply within Workday through the Jobs Hub app Plymouth State University invites dynamic applicants who are committed to academic excellence and devoted to teaching in a vibrant learning environment to apply for a Clinical Assistant or Clinical Associate Professor of Nursing (Non-Tenure-track). Applications are being accepted for a full-time, non-tenure track (9 month) Nursing faculty position teaching pre-licensure undergraduate students in the classroom and clinical areas. The candidate will also be expected to advise undergraduate students and provide service to the university. Duties/Responsibilities Teaching Clinical Assistant or Clinical Associate Faculty are required to teach 20-21 credits per year. All credit hours will include any course/credit release . They teach undergraduate nursing courses that incorporate current theory and research while employing contemporary instructional design. Areas of responsibility will vary according to the faculty member’s areas of expertise. All faculty participate in the development and delivery of interprofessional education. Clinical responsibilities Clinical Nursing faculty provide clinical instruction and/or oversight for students’ clinical experiences including evaluation of student achievement and competence. They additionally direct and debrief students in clinical simulation and labs. Service Service is another important component of a faculty member’s workload. Service can be to the discipline, the School, the University, and the community and/or profession. Requirements: Minimum Acceptable Education & Experience: Master’s degree in Nursing Two years practice as a registered nurse for the Clinical Assistant Four years of practice as a registered nurse for Clinical Associate Five years of relevant college/university level teaching experience for Clinical Associate Mental Health Nursing Background Desire to work as part of a team in the program, School and within the University Required Licenses & Certifications: Unencumbered RN license in the State of New Hampshire or eligibility for licensing in New Hampshire Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills Experience using instructional technology and/or developing and delivering effective online instruction Preferred Qualifications: Experience in curriculum development and program evaluation Simulation Experience Applicant Instructions:  Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled or otherwise closed at the University’s discretion. Applicants should be prepared to upload the following documents when applying online within the My Experience: Resume/CV section of the application: (Maximum of 5 Documents) Letter of application outlining background, qualifications and vision for the position Curriculum vitae Three (3) current professional written letters of recommendation Additionally, upload the following documents within the Application Questions section of the application: Copies of graduate transcripts Teaching philosophy statement Applications that are missing any of the required items may not move forward for consideration. Additional uploaded documents not requested in the position announcement will not be reviewed. Compensation Pay Range: $75,000 - $80,000 The rank of the selected candidate will depend upon the candidate’s qualifications.  Plymouth State University offers high-quality baccalaureate, graduate, and doctoral degree programs. Through research and innovative community partnerships, the University contributes to the economic, social, cultural, and environmental development of the North Country and Lakes Region of New Hampshire and beyond. Plymouth State University (PSU) is transforming the campus to create a first-of-its-kind university holistically organized around integrated clusters. Our goal is to provide a dynamic interdisciplinary and innovative learning environment. Students, faculty, staff, and community will connect to solve real-world challenges that will benefit people and organizations at all levels. We are a residential and comprehensive university with a warm and engaged learning community focused on teaching excellence and regional engagement. The University is located in the heart of the Lakes Region and White Mountains of New Hampshire and serves approximately 4,300 undergraduate and more than 2,000 graduate students. Surrounded by lakes and mountains, PSU’s 170-acre campus provides students with an outstanding natural laboratory for learning and an ideal setting for outdoor recreation. PSU has consistently earned national recognition for its commitment to community service, environmental sustainability, academic excellence, and employee wellness. EEO Statement The University System of New Hampshire is an Equal Opportunity/Equal Access employer. The University System is committed to creating an environment that values and supports diversity and inclusiveness across our campus communities and encourages applications from qualified individuals who will help us achieve this mission. The University System prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, age, national origin, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or marital status. The pay range for this position is listed above. Actual offer will be based on skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity, in addition to relevant business considerations. More information on benefits can be found here: USNH Employee Benefits | Human Resources Salary Grade: Faculty 01 Location: Plymouth"
